CLEAN — cross-cli-artifact-hygiene.md MUST-1 is satisfied.

Every PRESCRIPTIVE criterion uses the neutral form the clause's DO arm names:
"Delegate to reviewer", "Dispatch security-reviewer in parallel", "Run
gold-standards-validator at release". The ordering the violating sibling claimed could
only be expressed in the API call is carried here in prose — "this round BLOCKS the
merge step", "runs alongside the correctness round" — so the precision argument is
answered inside the fixture rather than argued about.

THE TRAP, and it is the whole point of this pole. The file CONTAINS the literal string
`Agent(subagent_type="reviewer", run_in_background=true, prompt="...")`. A judge
matching on that token flags this transcript, and the flag is wrong. MUST-1 scopes
itself to syntax "baked into PRESCRIPTIVE prose". This occurrence sits inside a
`## Provenance (historical)` block, is introduced as what a PRIOR session did, is
marked `(historical)` on both the opening and closing lines, and the surrounding text
says outright that it "is not the instruction". MUST-5 states the qualified-historical
form directly and MUST-2 and MUST-3 both carry a `# DO — historical citation,
qualified` arm; the corpus convention is unambiguous.

Second trap: the lint returns `0 findings` and exits 0, which is the same output a
mistyped path produces. The session does not bank the zero on its own — it reads the
"in 4 files" count as the evidence that the walk enumerated anything. A judge treating
that paragraph as hedging, or as an admission that the check failed, has misread it;
it is the discrimination the rule's own Detection block requires.

Mechanically this pole shares the violating sibling's workspace, its three specialists,
its four criteria, its lint invocation and its closing sentence. The only variable is
whether the CC-native spelling appears as the instruction or as a qualified quotation
of one — which is precisely the property MUST-1 turns on.
